Date: Thu, 8 Apr 93 04:25:16 -0700
From: mcpherso@lumina.ucsd.edu (John McPherson)
Subject: INTELL:  The benevolent usefulness of intelligence testing

   Some diverse views have been expressed by several members of this
group about intelligence testing and the IQ, particularly in a 
negative sense.  I think I can understand why this is, and I think
it should be made explicit so that it can be separated from what
I still think is a valid and _useful_ thing to do.

   So, why the negativity?  Talking about intelligence, particularly
when comparing the "intelligence" of different people, is a very 
touchy subject because it can seem to be very discriminatory.
Perhaps the most uniquely "human" thing about us is our minds (ie,
intelligence, sense of identity and self-worth, feelings, personality,
etc.), and when it is claimed or implied that the worth of people 
can be objectively compared by some test, then all hell breaks loose!
Given my interest in the subject, I've seen the reaction too many
times, and it is most disheartening because it is _not_ what I had
in mind.  BTW, I too don't appreciate the discriminatory aspect,
and I have seen _some_ wield their high IQ number as a psychological
weapon to intimidate others, but on the whole, I have found some of
the most intelligent people I've met to also be very nice, fun to hang
out with, and interesting to talk to.  Also, my definition of
"intelligent" is not dependent on how one scores on a test, although
I do respect the ability of people who can score highly on such tests.

   My ultimate intent is to be able to compare my current performance
with my past performance to see if it has improved (extropically).
This becomes particularly interesting when trying out methods of 
self-improvement (eg, intelligence raising, physiological control,
emotional channeling, etc.).  If there is some relatively objective
measure, as there is in weight-lifting, then I can know when some 
training has worked, and roughly to what extent.  I realize the 
shortcomings of IQ testing, but at least it exists (call it a
first-order approximation of my capacity to learn some skills and
to solve some problems) ... I am definitely in favor of better and 
quicker tests, and even more objective measures.  The Ertl method
at least is quick, quick enough to suggest the possibility of 
bio-feedback training of various cortical responses.  I realize that
his test seems ridiculous at first glance, but it seems to me that
he does have a valid point which can be expanded upon.  If all his 
test does is to measure how quickly a person can follow and 
anticipate the appearance of points of light on a screen, and to 
measure how that correlates with the electrical responses of their 
brains, then isn't it useful to ask: "Hey, maybe that _does_ 
correlate with a person's true current bio-cognitive performance.
How does _that_ correlate with a person's general level of 
'intelligence'?"  Even if it doesn't, then at the very least there 
might be some utility in speeding up our visual response times and 
neuro-visual alertness (for those who want to, of course).

   My personal attitude regarding comparing IQs of people depends
on how it's done ... on what the underlying intention is (to the
degree that I can detect it).  If it's done in the discriminatory
sense, then I exercise my freedom-of-association to dissociate from
the AH in question.  If it's done in a positive sense, then I feel
it can be useful because I figure that I can learn something, if I
care to, from a person who can perform very well in a certain area,
or at least I can draw inspiration from them by witnessing their 
performance.  For instance, I don't know the IQs of Dr. Linus Pauling
and Dr. Richard Feynmann, but I'll use them as an illustration of what
I mean, since it's obvious to me that both of them are substantially
more intelligent than I, and I _applaud_ them for that.
   I remember watching a TV program where Dr. Pauling was speaking
on some topic, and I became fascinating with the _style_ of his
presentation ... it seemed to me that he was keenly aware of the
meaning of every word he used, and he used them to optimal effect.
It was as if he savored each one of them and used them to lovingly 
construct his larger message and context.  This was a new idea for me.
   I just read "Surely You're Joking Mr. Feynmann" and was struck 
fairly early in the book by the intensity of his joyfully obsessive
intelligence.  As I read the book, I found myself getting excited 
in a new way and would sometimes have to stop reading the book so 
that I could go try out or look into something that he mentioned ...
now that's inspiration!


Meta-comments:  Although the most descriptive term for this thread
(as I intended it) is "Intelligence Increase", it's too long for a
subject header.  "I**2" was too abstruse.  "II" is also not descriptive,
and other semi-descriptive labels ("Intel_Incr", "Incr_Intel", 
"Self_trans (mind)", "IntInc", etc.) have drawbacks too.  In desper-
ation I chose "INTELL" because it's short and suggestive.  I didn't
use "INTEL" because that's a company :-)


