Date: Thu, 08 Apr 93 09:23:29 -0400
From: "W. Scott Meeks" <meeks@osf.org>
Subject: EVOL: New forms overtaking old (was JAPAN: GDP and attitudes)

>Date: Wed, 7 Apr 93 17:40:17 EDT
>From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
>   From: "W. Scott Meeks" <meeks@osf.org>
[irrelevencies elided]
>The introduction of a new form of evolution does not eliminate or
>invalidate any other.  Our memes have been evolving for, what?, 10,000
>years, and so have our genes.  Their interactions are non-trivial,
>somewhat like the way that the genes of fleas interact with the genes
>of dogs...

Your second and third sentences are irrelevant.

Your first sentence may be true, though there is some evidence that
biological evolution may have wiped out it's precursor.  I don't know if
you have read "Blind Watchmaker" in addition to "Selfish Gene" but in it
Dawkins advances the theory that a form of inorganic self-replication in
certain clay-like materials may have provided some sort of precursor or
jumping-off point for organic self-replicating molecules.  Once the organic
molecules became dominant, the conditions for the inorganic molecules to
continue to evolve were destroyed.  I'll look up the reference this evening
to confirm my memory.

Regardless, I think the real point is not that new forms of evolution
invalidate or eliminate old, but that newer forms have greater effects and
thus wind up mostly swamping the effects of prior forms.  In particular, I
think there is considerable evidence that memetic evolution is swamping
genetic evolution.

