Date: Thu, 8 Apr 1993 08:00:45 -0700
From: D Anton Sherwood <dasher@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: gene-mangling

This is to Andrew Hall, but it's of interest to the list.

Looking again at our conversation of two weeks ago (is it only two weeks?
time goes funny in cyberspace) I see that you may have misunderstood my
intention.  I wasn't talking about using a genetic editor to change my hair
color.  

Someone brought up the idea of a targeted plague, i.e. a contagious agent
that is (mostly) harmless to everyone except the victim's unique genotype.
(Thus some jokes about voodoo.)

My claim was that the very fact that makes such an attack possible, human
genetic diversity, makes it gedanken-easy to evade -- there's lots of
genotype-space to run around in.  If your messages to me about the potential
dangers of naive gene-butchery touch that point, I'm too naive to see how.

Here's a somewhat weaker defense than I had in mind, but which illustrates
the point.  Assuming the attacker wants me, and not my parents, siblings or
offspring, changing half my DNA will save me.  So I get a sample cell from
the next willing male stranger I meet, sequence its DNA, and set nanites to
simulating meiotic crossover with that gene-set in each of my cells.  (My
cells become gametes of a virtual tetraploid beast -- and I become a son of
two fathers, one of whom is my former self.)  Very long experience suggests
there's a very high probability that the resulting gene-set will be viable.

