Date: Thu, 8 Apr 1993 08:01:27 -0700
From: D Anton Sherwood <dasher@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: so, why are we clever?

It's been mentioned a couple of times that intelligence is correlated with
speedy nerves.  Well, I can't disagree; I'm clever, and unusually agile at
catching things that are about to fall.  (One time as I lifted a watermelon
out of the refrigerator with both hands, I dislodged a pitcher; I trapped
the pitcher with my left leg, spilling nothing.)

Is the correlation causal?  Not obvious how.  Sure, we may think faster than
our neighbors, but that's not by a long shot all that's distinctive about
us; it's obviously not why we chose each other as pen-pals!

A speedier brain has time left over, in some sense; but for what?  It's not
a time-sharing stored-program computer; I can't swap out the part of my mind
that handles walking or heartbeats and use it for crossword puzzles.

Could it be that a speedier brain is easier to rewire?  Maybe that's where
the idle time comes in handy -- if the duty cycle is low, there's more
*opportunity* to rewire.

Perhaps causality is the other way around -- a brain with high self-tuning
ability will make itself smarter *and* faster.

