Subject: Martin Gardner on Korzybski

On Wed, 7 Apr 1993, I151000 wrote:

>   Mr. Gardener mentions that attempting to write in E-Prime gives him a
> headache, and apparently he thinks that should give eveyone else a head-
> ache too. Breaking out of the Aristotlean logic system where he can be
> right, and everyone else is wrong seems to be a problem for him.  This
> seems to be a big problem for Gardner, Randi and the rest of the
> finger pointers at CSICOP.

    ...

> our brains and ways of thinking.  To read Gardener's naive and
> intellectually thin "debunking" of Korzybski's work and assume that he
> and Neurolinguistics, General Semantics, and Neurolinguistic Programming
> had nothing to offer would be passing up a wealth of information on
> boundless expansion and self improvement.

Yes, well that's Gardner for you.  He doesn't just debunk something,
he debunks the HELL out of it.  (If you're down on Garder and his
CSICOP gang, you'll enjoy the delightful caricature of Gardner, Sagan, and
some others in RAWilson's _The Widow's Son_). 

Korzybski is easy to make fun of.  He had a lot of the tunnel
vision and megalomaniacal tendencies typical of crackpots.  For
Gardner this automatically eliminates the possibility of there being
anything worthwhile in his work.  Since the man is so obviously a crank,
there's no point in looking at his ideas to see if there's anything of
value (not to mention that if you're seen looking people might think
that you're a crank, or worse, a dupe of cranks).  The same line of
thinking leads to automatic dismissal of Reich (who made some
pretty loony claims about orgone energy, UFO's, and what-all in his
later years), or for that matter of Ayn Rand.  

IMO just because the originator of an idea (or his enthusiasts) may 
succumb to the delusion that his idea holds the Answer to Everything 
and start babbling like a subway schizophrenic doesn't automatically mean
that it was a crazy idea to start with.  But you can't pursue this 
notion if you're too worried about being judged by the intellectual
company you keep, and (at risk of too much amateur psychologizing)
I think this may be part of Gardner's problem.  

(As a picky aside, there is a valid, respectable, 100% scientific
discipline called neurolinguistics, a branch of neuropsychology,
which involves actual brain research.  Real Neurolinguists get
*very* testy about being linked with NLP, GS, or anything of the
kind.  It would be fairer to leave them out of this kind of 
discussion, and since they had the word first, to leave it out
too).

Scott DeLancey



