Nick Szabo:

> 
> I wonder how much several modern schools of thought borrow from GS
> (General Semantics), or whether there is a common ancestor. 
> 
> For example, cognitive science seems in many ways similar to GS.
> Cognitive therapy is the first form of "talk therapy" that has
> been demonstrated to have a significant effect treating depression.
> It's as effective as psychoactive drugs like Prozac; overall a 
> combination of drug & cognitive therapy is often most effecitve.

   [description of cognitive therapy deleted]

> Much of that sounds straight out of Korzybski, but specifically targetted
> towards depression.  The only thing missing is that the cognitive
> scientists don't credit Korzybski, thus I wonder whether the two
> have a common ancestor or if Korzybski is being unjustly snubbed.
> In any case, I think the NLP folks should dump the last vestiges
> Freud and start talking to the cognitive folks.

I just want to delineate some academic turf here.  I don't know
anything about cognitive therapy, including whether it is based
on or has anything to do with Cognitive Science.  (I doubt it,
though, since Cognitive Science, which has only been on the scene
for 10-15 years, has yet to produce much of anything that would
make for the basis of useful therapy).  I do know a lot of 
cognitive scientists, however (under favorable conditions of
lighting I sometimes even pass for one myself).  Few if any show
much interest in therapeutic techniques (except possibly in
a few cases as consumers).  Cognitive therapists may well be
onto something, and may for all I know be scientific as all
get-out, but cognitive scientists are something else.  (There
are even a couple of Cognitive Science departments around the
country--one at UC San Diego, another I think at Brown, probably
