Date: Thu, 8 Apr 93 11:25:18 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: FTL: traversable wormholes

As Mike Price has mentioned, he and I have been privately discussing
and researching wormholes and "boundless" expansion.  Since the
wormhole discussion has been opened here, let me step in and discuss
that, and post on the boundless stuff later.

Anyway, I would have titled this thread "Wormhole Empires", because
the potential and even need for central control is dramatic, and may
be a good example of one of those upcoming hard problems David
Friedman warns us about.

Imagine a wormhole or set of wormholes which approach allowing some
closed causal loop to exist as some complex path through them.  If the
result is that the holes simply repel each other, that would be nice,
and offer lots of interesting possibilities for mechanical devices.
But if instead, as I guess, all these holes suddenly collapse, then
global coordination would be required to manage a network of holes
across spacetime.  All the holes with one end at the center and other
ends accelerating away in the same direction would have to all
accelerate at about the same rate.  And if anyone sent a hole end back
toward the center, watch out!  Not only does that close the airports,
the airports may explode with huge force.  (Such airports are best
left in away from the center of town!)

Terrorists with hole technology would be a grave danger, so that hole
technology might be treated the way nukes are now, with some
centralized control and monitoring over who gets and uses it.  The
topology of holes would have to be designed not only to allow a
minimum average number of jumps per trip, but also so that a minimum
number of travel holes are destroyed by a rogue hole.

A sign of two civilizations meeting would then be massive destruction
of holes along a growing frontier area.  And even one or two holes
could serve as very efficient alien detectors, detecting any holes
moving in other directions over a *very* large volume.  Very careful
coordination would be required for the hole networks to merge, as Mike
speculates is possible.  And with massive hole destruction, arranging
meetings for negotiations might take a while in empire time.  Here's a
good Schelling point problem - where should hole ends meet to merge
the nets?

Finally, the spatial center of the empire would have strong military
advantages, like the top of a steep and very tall mountain now, even
if the empire allowed holes between other pairs of places.  Imagine a
colony 100 light years from the center (in galactic co-moving
coordinates), but so that if you stepped into the center end of a hole
at the same time you sent out a light signal, you would arrive at the
outpost end of the hole one year before the light signal arrived
there.  Then if the colony declared war on the center, the center
could launch weapons (through ordinary space) to arrive at the colony
in one year of empire time, but a weapon from the colony would take
almost 200 years of empire time to reach the center!  And the ratio
gets worse as the speed with which the hole ends were sent out
increases.

Of course weapons could get places faster if sent through holes, but
those would be carefully guarded.  For peacetime trading, a tremendous
"time wind" (wind of change?) would allow much faster velocities
(relative to empire time) for moving mass out from the center,
compared with moving mass toward the center.  This would limit how
fast the center could grow physically.  I don't think much net mass
can be sent through the holes without destroying them.

Robin Hanson

